      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Einstein hated the idea that a measurement here could instantly decide something far away, because it seemed to clash with his belief that nothing can travel faster than light—so he called it “spooky action at a distance.” He thought the theory must be incomplete, hoping hidden variables would restore a local, deterministic picture. But experiments (starting with Aspect’s in the 1980s) showed the spooky correlations really do exist, and they can’t be explained by any signal traveling between the particles, which is why the phrase still feels unsettling.",
